      "id": "tp11db8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Foundation of the Delhi Sultanate"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Delhi Sultanate consisted of five dynasties: Mamluk (Slave), Khilji, Tughlaq, Sayyid, and Lodi.",
        "Qutb-ud-din Aibak founded the Mamluk Dynasty in 1206 CE and initiated the construction of the Qutub Minar.",
        "Iltutmish consolidated the empire, introduced the Iqta system, and formed the Turkan-i-Chahalgani (Group of 40).",
        "Alauddin Khilji implemented strict market control regulations, fixed prices, and introduced the Dagh (branding of horses) and Chehra systems.",
        "Muhammad bin Tughlaq is remembered for ambitious experiments including the token currency and shifting the capital to Daulatabad.",
        "The Delhi Sultanate came to an end in 1526 when Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi at the First Battle of Panipat."
      ],
      "id": "i9gmssn"
    },
